# /etc/sudoers.d/bubble-cockpit-approver
# Board #1432 (C2) — cockpit-approver minter grant for the console's `claude` user.
#
# Lets the `claude` user (the console's systemd User=, see
# console/deploy/bubble-ops-console.service.template) run the ROOT-owned
# `bubble-cockpit-approver-token.sh` minter, which needs root to read
# /etc/age/key.txt (0400 root) and decrypt the SOPS-encrypted cockpit-approver
# App private key. Mirrors the existing scoped grant convention used for the
# settings_pr broker-mint wrapper (deploy/templates/bubble-broker-mint.sudoers):
# ONE exact absolute binary path, no args wildcard, no environment passthrough.
#
# NOTE (found during the #1432 button/page build, worth resolving before this
# actually goes live): the production console unit sets NoNewPrivileges=true
# (console/deploy/bubble-ops-console.service.template). Under
# NoNewPrivileges=true, `sudo` — a setuid-root binary — cannot escalate, so a
# request-time `sudo -n` call from the console process (as
# console/services/pr_approver.py's `_mint_token()` does today) will fail in
# production exactly like it would for the contents-token flow — which is
# precisely why THAT minter (console/deploy/contents-token/) was built instead
# as a root-owned systemd TIMER writing a tmpfs file the console only READS,
# never sudos for. This grant is shipped because the task asked for a sudo-n
# grant mirroring the broker-mint convention, but flip NoNewPrivileges or
# migrate pr_approver.py to the timer+tmpfs-file pattern before relying on it
# — verify with `sudo -n -l -U claude` and a live smoke-test (see the
# cockpit-approver README) before treating this as load-bearing.
#
# Deploy: validate with `visudo -cf bubble-cockpit-approver.sudoers`, then
# `install -m 0440 -o root -g root` to /etc/sudoers.d/bubble-cockpit-approver
# (see console/deploy/cockpit-approver/install-cockpit-approver-minter.sh).
claude ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/bubble-cockpit-approver-token.sh
